index 0000000..c6811a7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/adr/0015-append-only-consent-records.md
@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+# ADR-0015: Consent records are append-only rows in `identity.consent`
+
+- **Status:** Proposed
+- **Date:** 2026-11-04
+- **Deciders:** <names>
+- **Sources:** T3-AC-004, T3-D-009, architecture section 4 (`identity` owns consents, emits "consent changed"), TD-08, TD-09, AN-900
+
+## Context
+
+Head office must prove each consent of a person, for example for marketing e-mails (T4 privacy controls). T3-AC-004 asks that a consent record stores the person, the purpose, the version of the consent text, the channel (`web`, `advisor` or `import`), the decision (given or withdrawn) and the time of the decision. Records are never changed or deleted; a new decision adds a record. The `identity` module owns consents (architecture section 4). Other modules, for example `journey` before it sends a message, must learn about each change of a consent. This adds a table to the data model and an event to the contract of `identity`.
+
+## Options
+
+1. **One mutable row per person and purpose.** Update the row on each decision. Good: the current decision is one lookup. Bad: the history is lost, so head office cannot prove a past consent; breaks T3-AC-004.
+2. **Mutable current row plus a history table.** Good: fast current read. Bad: two writes that can disagree; the current row is changed, which T3-AC-004 forbids.
+3. **Append-only records.** One row per decision in `identity.consent`. The current decision is the latest row per person and purpose. Good: the table is the proof; one write; matches T3-AC-004. Bad: the current read needs "latest per purpose".
+
+## Decision
+
+We choose option 3. The table `identity.consent` has the columns `id`, `person_id`, `purpose`, `text_version`, `channel`, `granted` and `decided_at`. The repository offers only insert and reads; it has no update or delete. Production adds a database rule that rejects `UPDATE` and `DELETE` on the table. The order of records is `decided_at`, then `id` (UUIDv7). After each insert, `identity` publishes `identity.consent-changed` with the payload `personId`, `consentId` and `purpose`. The payload holds no decision, because a consent choice is personal data (data conventions); a reacting module reads the decision through `identity.currentConsent`.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The proof of consent is the table itself; the history is complete by construction.
+- The current read is "latest record per person and purpose". An index on `(person_id, purpose, decided_at)` keeps it fast.
+- `granted` is personal data (category `preference`, retention `account lifetime + 3 years`).
+- The erasure of a person (later story) must decide how the proof of consent is kept for its retention; this ADR does not decide it.
+- A second brand (T3-D-013) may need consents per brand. The purpose key can carry it; a later ADR decides.
+- Tests: `packages/core/src/identity/consent.test.ts` (T3-AC-004). `pnpm pii` checks the column metadata.
